# .skill-lint.conf — per-repo extension for skill-lint.
#
#   cp templates/skill-lint.conf.example <your-repo>/.skill-lint.conf
#
# Place it at the repo root. skill-lint finds it by walking up from the skills
# directory to the git root. A repo may only ADD rules with this file; it can
# never relax the baseline bar (docs/skill-quality.md). Errors are never
# disablable — only warnings, and only per skill.
#
# FORMAT: one directive per line. Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are
# ignored. Parsed in bash 3.2 — no YAML, no quoting.

# forbid-pattern <ERE> <message>
#   Fail (error) when the extended regular expression matches a SKILL.md body
#   (everything after the frontmatter). The pattern is the first field and must
#   contain no spaces — everything after it is the message. Use [[:space:]] to
#   match whitespace.
#
#   Ban a placeholder left in from the template:
#forbid-pattern TODO:? body still contains a TODO marker
#   Ban an absolute home path leaking into a skill:
#forbid-pattern /home/[a-z]+/ hard-coded home path — read paths from the environment

# require-field <frontmatter-key>
#   Fail (error) when a skill's frontmatter lacks this key. Use it to make a
#   Claude Code extension field mandatory in a repo that relies on it.
#
#require-field allowed-tools

# ignore-warn <skill-name> <check-id>
#   Silence one WARNING for one skill, by criterion ID (see docs/skill-quality.md).
#   Errors can never be silenced this way; naming an error's ID here is inert.
#
#   Let one skill keep its deliberately terse description:
#ignore-warn my-terse-skill SQ7
